+## 3. Task 3 — Comparative disposition: more permissive, more restrictive, or comparable?
+
+### 3a. The answer
+
+⭐⭐⭐ **MORE PERMISSIVE, AND BY A WIDE MARGIN, ON EVERY AXIS THIS PASS COULD TEST. ⚠️ THIS IS THE FINDING THAT RUNS AGAINST JD'S POSITION AND IT IS STATED WITHOUT HEDGING.**
+
+| Axis | Church of England, c. 1899 | Protestant Episcopal Church, c. 1899 |
+|---|---|---|
+| **Ceremonial law** | Ornaments Rubric, statutory via the Act of Uniformity 1662; Canon 36 of 1604; the 1559 Act's *"none other or otherwise"* | ⛔ **None.** No Ornaments Rubric, no ceremonial law of any kind, on its own High Churchmen's admission |
+| **Courts with jurisdiction over ritual** | Court of Arches; Judicial Committee of the Privy Council; the Public Worship Regulation Act 1874's machinery | ⛔ **None comparable.** Diocesan ecclesiastical courts only, under a canon whose test was doctrinal |
+| **Restrictive canon in force** | Yes, and statutory | 1874 ritual canon, **repealed 1904** |
+| **Enforcement record** | Ritualist prosecutions; clergy imprisoned under the PWRA; *Martin v. Mackonochie*, *Hebbert v. Purchas*, *Read v. Bishop of Lincoln* | ⛔ **One admonishment, ever** (Prescott, 1877). **No trial concluded against a ritualist** |
+| **Metropolitical determination on incense** | The 1899 Opinion, followed by the 1901 joint episcopal letter binding the bench | ⛔ **None found** |
+| **Direction of travel, 1899–1904** | Tightening: Opinion 1899, episcopal letter 1901, Royal Commission 1904–06 | ⭐ **Loosening: ritual canon repealed 1904** |
+
+⛔⛔ **THE TWO CHURCHES MOVED IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS ACROSS EXACTLY THE PERIOD IN QUESTION. That is the comparative finding, and it is not a favourable one for treating 1899 as an Anglican-wide statement.**
+
+### 3b. The American context, stated fairly — it was contested, not placid
+
+⭐ **It would be false to present the American church as untroubled about ritualism, and this file does not.** The record includes: Bishop Eastburn's years-long refusal to visit the Church of the Advent; the strong and vocal evangelical opposition; the 1871 conventional resolutions and episcopal pastoral condemning the new ritualism; the 1873 Reformed Episcopal schism; the 1874 canon; the blocking of DeKoven's election as bishop **twice** — Wisconsin 1874 and Illinois 1875 — by dioceses that refused to ratify him, on account of his eucharistic theology as exposed by his ritual advocacy; and repeated attempts in General Convention to legislate against ritual "excesses."
+
+⛔⛔ **BUT THE STRUCTURAL POINT SURVIVES ALL OF IT, AND IT IS THE POINT: EVERY AMERICAN RESTRAINT WAS DOCTRINAL, EPISCOPAL AND LARGELY UNENFORCED, WHILE THE ENGLISH RESTRAINTS WERE LEGAL, JUDICIAL AND OCCASIONALLY PENAL.** ⭐ **An American ritualist in 1899 faced hostile bishops, hostile conventions and a blocked path to the episcopate. He did not face a court that could deprive him or a statute that made his ceremonial unlawful.**
+
