+### 4b. ⛔⛔ On the 1899 Opinion and on incense, the EMC has no recorded position — and this is a hard negative, not a soft one
+
+**The EMC's Constitution and Canons were read directly. The finding is structural and it is clean:**
+
+- **Title II of the Canons is "Worship." It contains exactly three canons: Canon 12, Of Translations of the Bible; Canon 13, Of the Standard Book of Common Prayer; Canon 14, Of the Music of the Church.** ⛔⛔ **There is no ceremonial canon, no ritual canon, and no canon capable of touching incense. The EMC's canonical provision for worship regulates the Bible version, the Prayer Book, and the music — and nothing else.**
+- **Canon 13 reads, in its entirety:** *"According to Article VII of the Constitution, the 1928 Book of Common Prayer shall be the standard Book of Common Prayer in this Church."* ⭐ **And per §2b above, the 1928 Book contains no Ornaments Rubric and no regnal-year formula — so the EMC's standard book is one from which the Opinion's entire subject matter is absent.**
+- **The EMC's own worship page states the church "predominantly uses *The Book of Common Prayer* (1928) as the basis for our worship"** and makes no reference to ceremonial, ritual, ornaments or incense.
