+## 7. ⭐⭐⭐ What can and cannot be safely claimed in conversation
+
+### 7a. Wording that will hold up
+
+⭐ **These will survive a well-informed challenge. The formulations are deliberate; the italicised words are load-bearing.**
+
+- *"The 1899 Opinion was given by the Archbishops of Canterbury and York on a reference from the Bishop of London under the English Prayer Book's own provision. Its holding is that liturgical incense is not enjoined or permitted by the law of the Church of England — that is the Opinion's own phrase for its own scope."*
+- *"Its reasoning is a construction of the Ornaments Rubric against the Acts of Uniformity of 1559 and 1662 and Canon 36 of 1604. The American Prayer Book has never contained the Ornaments Rubric — the 1928 Standard Book contains neither the rubric nor the regnal-year formula the whole argument turns on."*
+- *"American High Churchmen who wanted the rubric to apply in America conceded that it did so only as a matter of loyalty to the mother church, not of law — and granted in the same breath that the American church 'has persistently omitted to set forth any ceremonial law.'"*
+- *"Even in England the Opinion was not a court judgement and carried no coercive force; Archbishop Temple said so himself in the Lords, and it bound a clergyman only where his own diocesan took it up."*
+- *"The 1662's own section on ceremonies says that in these things the Church of England condemns no other nations and prescribes nothing but to its own people, and that every country should use such ceremonies as it thinks best."*
+- *"So far as I can find, the American House of Bishops never addressed the Opinion. Their 1901 and 1904 Pastoral Letters — the first two after it — don't mention it, and the Archbishop of Canterbury was at the 1904 Convention in person."*
+- *"The EMC's canonical provision for worship covers the Bible version, the Prayer Book and the music. There's no ceremonial canon and nothing on incense either way."*
+
+### 7b. ⛔⛔ Wording that will NOT hold up — do not use these
+
+- ⛔ *"The Anglican Communion ruled against incense in 1899."* **False.** Two English metropolitans gave a non-binding opinion about English law.
+- ⛔ *"The Lambeth Conference ruled…"* / *"the 1899 Lambeth ruling."* **⚠️ CATEGORY ERROR, AND THE BRIEF'S OWN TITLE INVITES IT. There was no Lambeth Conference in 1899.** The hearing was **at Lambeth Palace**; it was not a Conference act, not a pan-Anglican instrument, and not a court. ⭐ **Say "the 1899 Archbishops' Opinion." An informed interlocutor will seize on "Lambeth" and be right to.**
+- ⛔ *"Anglicans of that period held incense to be unlawful."* **Falsified by this pass.** In America it was practised in prominent parishes, defended by a Presiding Bishop in print from 1866, never the subject of a canon naming it, and the one restrictive canon was repealed in 1904.
+- ⛔ *"The American church accepted / declined to accept the Opinion."* ⚠️ **Neither is established. What is established is silence in the sources read** — and silence in two Pastoral Letters is not a decision. **Say "I can find no American response," and say what was and was not searched.**
+- ⛔ *"The EMC has no position on incense, so incense is foreign to its tradition."* ⚠️ **A non-sequitur, and its own "About" page cuts the other way** — it embraces styles "from Evangelical to Anglo-Catholic."
+- ⛔ Any quantitative claim about how many American parishes used incense. ⛔ **There is no such figure (§6 gap 4).**
+
+### 7c. ⚠️ The concession to make voluntarily, and early
+
+⚠️⚠️ **If the Opinion is raised at all, the honest position concedes the evidential half before being pushed off it.** Something in the shape of: *"I'd hold the Opinion is about English law and doesn't reach the American line — and I'd add that American practice at the time was more permissive than English, not less, so I wouldn't try to use it as evidence of what Anglicans generally thought. What it does bear on is narrower: what the 1662's ceremonial provision actually authorises. That's the ground I'd want to argue on."*
+
+⭐⭐ **Conceding the weaker half unprompted is what makes the stronger half credible, and the stronger half is genuinely strong.** ⛔ **Deploying the Opinion as a general Anglican verdict invites exactly the refutation §3a supplies, and loses the jurisdictional point along with it.**
